  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1146 PM CDT SUN SEP 13 2026\n\nThe weak upper-level trough continues to push off to the east. In\nturn, upper-level ridging is already beginning to establish\nitself across the southeast. By this time Monday, this building\nridge will be established over Tennessee, decreasing our rain\nchances through the week significantly. Rain chances will\ngenerally be low (<20%), with any isolated shower or storm being\ndiurnally driven in the afternoon hours each day.\n\nLatest CAMs show minimal rain chances in the far south and west\non Monday (<25%), warranting a slight upward adjustment in PoPs.\nAfternoon high temperatures will also quickly rebound by Monday,\nwith highs returning to the mid and upper-90s. While afternoon\nheat index values will generally reach above 100 degrees, no heat\nheadlines are anticipated given the lack of higher humidity;\nhowever, proper hydration and heat safety precautions are advised\nduring peak afternoon hours. High confidence remains in this\ngeneral forecast trend continuing into next weekend, as very\nlittle movement is expected from the upper-level ridge over the\nnext five days.\n\n16",
